WEBVTT

00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:06.340
Ask anyone in the Philippines how they get around,

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:09.541
and sooner or later, they'll mention the tricycle.

00:00:09.651 --> 00:00:14.014
Three wheels, a sidecar, and room for six people, driver included.

00:00:14.756 --> 00:00:15.816
In twenty twelve,

00:00:15.816 --> 00:00:20.433
the country had more than seven and a half million registered motor vehicles.

00:00:20.543 --> 00:00:24.308
Fifty-five per cent of them were motorcycles and tricycles.

00:00:24.418 --> 00:00:27.436
They're cheap, and a quick way to get from A to B.

00:00:27.464 --> 00:00:30.860
But they're also loud, dirty, and they spew black smoke.

00:00:32.044 --> 00:00:36.594
So the Asian Development Bank put electric tricycles, or e-trikes,

00:00:36.594 --> 00:00:39.455
on the streets of Manila and nearby provinces.

00:00:39.565 --> 00:00:41.435
At fifty-seven inches wide,

00:00:41.435 --> 00:00:44.781
an e-trike is slimmer than the average sixty-inch tricycle.

00:00:44.891 --> 00:00:48.278
It cuts emissions, earns drivers more, rides comfortably,

00:00:48.278 --> 00:00:50.449
runs quietly, and charges fast.

00:00:50.559 --> 00:00:53.899
Where a tricycle can burn up to fifty pesos of fuel,

00:00:53.899 --> 00:00:57.572
an e-trike needs only about ten pesos of electricity.

00:00:58.485 --> 00:01:01.118
But the e-trike had problems of its own.

00:01:01.228 --> 00:01:03.188
Maintenance is expensive.

00:01:03.188 --> 00:01:05.088
The cabin lacks safety features.

00:01:05.088 --> 00:01:07.318
There are no signs to guide passengers.

00:01:07.318 --> 00:01:08.736
And the list goes on.

00:01:08.846 --> 00:01:13.558
Of the twenty e-trikes deployed in Mandaluyong, only seven were still running.

00:01:14.756 --> 00:01:16.687
So, how do we fix this?

00:01:16.797 --> 00:01:18.933
We propose eight changes.

00:01:20.349 --> 00:01:20.927
One.

00:01:20.927 --> 00:01:23.079
Pick-ups happen in proper places.

00:01:23.079 --> 00:01:27.539
New waiting sheds give drivers a station to pick up and drop off passengers.

00:01:28.485 --> 00:01:28.945
Two.

00:01:28.945 --> 00:01:35.392
A fixed starting fare of six pesos, for everyone, keeping the e-trike one of the cheapest rides in town.

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:37.150
Three.

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:44.110
The daily boundary, what drivers pay the operator each day, drops by thirteen per cent, so drivers take home more.

00:01:45.264 --> 00:01:45.784
Four.

00:01:45.784 --> 00:01:49.214
Drivers learn how to look after the lithium-ion battery.

00:01:49.214 --> 00:01:53.346
Poor charging habits shorten its life, and replacements are costly.

00:01:54.417 --> 00:01:55.047
Five.

00:01:55.047 --> 00:02:01.471
The e-trike itself is redesigned, with a storage compartment, more floor space, and better safety features.

00:02:02.552 --> 00:02:03.188
Six.

00:02:03.188 --> 00:02:05.202
No more waiting for a full load.

00:02:05.202 --> 00:02:12.181
E-trikes leave within ten to fifteen minutes, full or not, and the countdown starts as soon as the first passenger boards.

00:02:15.264 --> 00:02:15.804
Seven.

00:02:15.804 --> 00:02:20.998
Clear signs and placards, so passengers always know where to go and how to ride.

00:02:21.874 --> 00:02:22.284
Eight.

00:02:22.284 --> 00:02:28.394
E-trikes are shared out equally each day, so every driver gets a fair schedule and a proper day off.

00:02:28.394 --> 00:02:32.206
If a driver works Sunday to Friday, Saturday is his rest day.

00:02:33.569 --> 00:02:36.015
Why does an organised system matter?

00:02:36.125 --> 00:02:38.935
Because a better vehicle only goes so far.

00:02:38.935 --> 00:02:44.250
A proper system is what keeps everything running, and it's the key to good public transport.
